  array (
    'title' => 'China Visa from Nigeria: Tourist & Business Guide',
    'category' => 'visas',
    'body_html' => '<p>China has become one of the most popular destinations for Nigerian travellers — whether you are heading to Guangzhou or Yiwu to source goods, attending the Canton Fair, visiting family, or simply exploring Beijing, Shanghai and the Great Wall. But China is a visa-required country for Nigerian passport holders, and the application is a formal, document-heavy process managed through a dedicated visa centre rather than directly at the embassy counter. Getting it right the first time saves you money, stress and a possible missed trip.</p><p>This guide walks you through how the China visa works for Nigerians, the difference between the main visa categories, the documents you should prepare, and the practical mistakes that trip people up. Visa rules are set by the Chinese government and can change at any time, so treat everything below as general guidance and always confirm the current requirements before you lodge your application.</p><h2>Understanding the main China visa types</h2><p>China uses a letter-based system for its visa categories. The two that matter most to ordinary Nigerian travellers are the <strong>L visa</strong> and the <strong>M visa</strong>.</p><ul><li><strong>L visa (tourism)</strong> — for holidays, sightseeing and visiting friends or family. This is what you apply for if you are going to see the country, attend a wedding, or visit relatives.</li><li><strong>M visa (business and trade)</strong> — for commercial activities: meeting suppliers, attending trade fairs like the Canton Fair, negotiating contracts, or inspecting goods. Most Nigerian traders sourcing from Guangzhou, Yiwu or Shenzhen travel on an M visa.</li></ul><p>There are others you may come across — the <strong>F visa</strong> for non-commercial exchanges and visits, the <strong>Z visa</strong> for employment, the <strong>X visa</strong> for study, and the <strong>Q visa</strong> for family reunion with Chinese residents. If you are not sure which category fits your trip, describe your purpose honestly and let the visa centre or your travel consultant guide you. Applying under the wrong category is a common reason for refusal.</p><h2>Where Nigerians apply</h2><p>China visa applications from Nigeria are handled through the Chinese Visa Application Service Centre (often abbreviated CVASC), not by walking into the embassy. The centre collects your documents, takes your biometrics (fingerprints) and forwards everything to the Chinese mission for a decision. You generally need to attend in person at least once for fingerprinting unless you fall under an exemption, so plan to be physically available during the application window.</p><p>Because the centre and embassy arrangements, opening hours and biometric rules are periodically updated, always check the current process before travelling to lodge. Book or confirm your appointment slot early — during peak trade-fair seasons the centres get very busy and slots fill up.</p><h2>Documents you should prepare</h2><p>While the exact checklist depends on your visa type and can be updated by the authorities, Nigerian applicants are typically asked to assemble the following. Prepare clean, complete copies — missing a single item usually means a return trip.</p><h3>Core documents for every applicant</h3><ul><li><strong>A valid passport</strong> with at least six months\' validity beyond your intended stay and at least two blank visa pages. A tattered or nearly full passport is a problem — renew it first.</li><li><strong>The completed visa application form</strong>, filled accurately and signed. China\'s form is detailed and asks about your travel history, employment and relatives; answer every field truthfully.</li><li><strong>A recent passport photograph</strong> meeting the specified size and background requirements. Photos that do not meet the spec are rejected at submission.</li><li><strong>Proof of legal residence or status in Nigeria</strong> if you are not a Nigerian citizen applying here.</li></ul><h3>For a tourist (L) visa</h3><ul><li>Confirmed round-trip flight reservations showing entry and exit.</li><li>Hotel bookings or proof of accommodation for the full stay.</li><li>A day-by-day itinerary of where you plan to go.</li><li>If visiting family or friends, an invitation letter from your host in China with their details and a copy of their ID or residence permit.</li></ul><h3>For a business (M) visa</h3><ul><li>An invitation letter from your Chinese business partner, supplier or the trade-fair organiser. This is the single most important document for traders — it should state the purpose of the visit, your relationship, the dates, and who covers your costs.</li><li>Evidence of your own business in Nigeria, such as CAC registration documents, and sometimes a cover letter from your company.</li><li>Proof of previous trade or dealings if you have them (past contracts, shipping documents, previous China visas).</li></ul><h3>Supporting financial and personal documents</h3><ul><li>Bank statements covering recent months to show you can fund the trip and that you have ties keeping you returning to Nigeria.</li><li>Evidence of employment or business activity.</li><li>Travel insurance where required.</li></ul><p>Never fabricate any document. Fake bank statements, forged invitation letters or invented itineraries are not only grounds for refusal — they can get you banned. Chinese missions verify invitations and have seen every trick. Honesty with a modest but genuine profile beats a fabricated impressive one every time. If you want to understand the broader patterns that lead to rejections, our guide on <a href="/visa">common reasons visa applications get refused</a> is worth reading before you lodge.</p><h2>The invitation letter: get this right</h2><p>For business travellers especially, the invitation letter carries enormous weight. A strong letter is on the Chinese company\'s letterhead, clearly identifies both parties, states exactly why you are visiting and for how long, and gives contact details the mission can verify. If your supplier is reluctant or vague about providing one, that is a warning sign — chase it down well in advance. For trade fairs, the official organiser usually issues invitation or registration documents; keep these safe and submit them.</p><h2>Single, double or multiple entry</h2><p>China visas can be issued for a single entry, double entry or multiple entries, and the validity and permitted stay per visit vary. Frequent traders often hope for a multiple-entry visa, but the decision rests entirely with the Chinese mission and is influenced by your travel history and the strength of your application. First-time applicants frequently receive a single-entry visa and build up to longer validity over subsequent trips. Do not assume you will get multiple entry — plan your trip around what you are actually granted.</p><h2>Processing and timing</h2><p>Processing times for China visas vary with the season, the category and the volume of applications, and the authorities do not guarantee a fixed turnaround. The safest approach is simple: apply well ahead of your intended travel date — several weeks is sensible, and longer during busy periods such as the spring and autumn Canton Fair. Never book non-refundable flights before your visa is in hand. We cannot quote you an exact processing time because it genuinely changes; always ask the visa centre for the current estimate when you lodge.</p><h2>Practical tips for Nigerian applicants</h2><ul><li><strong>Match your documents to your story.</strong> Your flights, hotels, invitation and bank balance should all point to the same coherent trip. Inconsistencies invite suspicion.</li><li><strong>Show strong ties to Nigeria.</strong> A job, a registered business, family and property all signal that you intend to return. This matters for tourist visas in particular.</li><li><strong>Keep your passport in good shape.</strong> Six months\' validity and blank pages are not optional.</li><li><strong>Be reachable.</strong> The mission may call to verify details. Make sure the phone numbers on your invitation and forms actually work.</li><li><strong>Keep copies of everything.</strong> Scan and store your full application in case you need to reference or re-lodge it.</li><li><strong>Plan your sourcing trip around real appointments.</strong> If you are a trader, line up your supplier meetings and fair registration so your itinerary is genuine and verifiable.</li></ul><h2>After you arrive</h2><p>Once in China, carry your passport and visa at all times, respect the permitted length of stay stamped on entry, and register your accommodation where local rules require it (hotels usually handle this automatically, but if you stay privately you may need to register with the local police). Overstaying — even by a day — carries fines and can wreck future applications, so note your exit date carefully.</p><p>The China visa is detailed but entirely achievable when you approach it methodically: pick the correct category, assemble genuine documents, secure a solid invitation, and apply with time to spare. Thousands of Nigerian traders and tourists do it every year.</p><p>When you are ready to plan the trip, GoGlobia can help you organise your China application and book your flights and stays around your appointment dates — start on our <a href="/visa/china">China visa page</a>, and remember our team is available 24/7 to guide you through every step. Travel prepared, and let us handle the logistics.</p>',
